sol (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `sol session`: which desktop this machine starts, and a console picker
    at logout for machines without a display manager — the job of a greeter,
    done by the console you were already on. Sessions run in a transient
    systemd scope, and graphical-session.target is stopped after each one,
    so a session that ends is really over, whoever started its pieces.
  * Living beside KDE: sol doctor now names user units enabled into
    graphical-session.target that duplicate sol's own bar and notifier —
    the "extra header" every desktop was getting — and portal backends the
    conf names but the machine lacks. The package Recommends
    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr and -gtk, and install.sh warns when they are
    missing.
  * A logout stays a logout: the picker ends the login rather than
    restarting the desktop when it has no terminal to ask on or nothing to
    choose between, a typo at it keeps what you had, the spin guard counts
    only sessions that die within seconds of starting, and a saved pick no
    longer applies under a display manager, which already asked.
  * Session entries honor TryExec, so a half-uninstalled desktop leaves the
    picker; gdm is no longer accused of needing an X server its greeter
    does not want; driftwm is required only by the sessions that run it.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 02:30:00 +0000

sol (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sol is a desktop you can choose. A wayland-session entry puts it in the
    login screen beside every other desktop on the machine, so switching to
    sol and back is picking a different session rather than booting specially.
  * graphical-session.target is started, through a sol-session.target that
    depends on it — portals, notification daemons and keyring agents are
    wired to that target and were never starting.
  * Portals are told which backend this desktop uses, and told under the
    right name: the value that decides is the one the session manager holds,
    and driftwm sets its own for the processes it spawns.
  * sol doctor reports all three, and reports the desktop name portals will
    actually read rather than the one in the caller's environment.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0000

sol (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * focus: ask the windows, not only the screen. A client declares a minimum
    size and driftwm clamps a Resize to it, so a window asked for a twelfth of
    a screen can commit three times that and sit across its neighbours —
    twelve real applications was twenty-four overlaps. Terminals shrink to
    almost nothing and hid this completely. sol now reads back what was
    actually committed, remembers it against the app_id, and lays out again
    with fewer and larger tiles: one extra pass the first time an application
    is met, and none ever after.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0000

sol (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * The solar system as a workspace model for driftwm: districts, the bar,
    the overview, the context menu, drag-between-planets and the shader.
  * Screen modes — focus, solo, present — plus the night and dark palettes.
    These need a driftwm whose IPC has a Resize, and say so when it does not.
  * Installed as a module with a stub on the path, so Python caches its
    compiled form rather than re-parsing on every keypress.

 -- sol <noreply@github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000
